From 2b83ebedf07cacce6fc110a45725f58e634ae522 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: VCKing101 <59761021+VCKing101@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:32:33 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 48/62] Frame pacing: presents between flips fall in the idle
 time

With frame interpolation the presents are made on the emulation thread.
The one between two frames fell a few milliseconds after a flip, while the
game's code built its next frame, and waited for the next thread switch:
up to 7 ms late, so presents came 11 and 22 ms apart (a 1% low of 45 fps
on a steady 60, the "45" in the frame-time readout).

PresentClock::note_idle: the first idle time after a flip (the kernel's
idle hook) ends the frame's work, and the delay allows for it, so both
presents of a frame fall in the idle time (8 ms more delay on the PC).
Code that leaves less idle time than the presents' spacing keeps the
flip's time: a longer delay would not help. And the runtime's starvation
boundaries poll for due presents too, for long stretches of guest code.

<prefix>_PRESENT_AFTER_FLIP counts the work to the flip, and
<prefix>_NO_CODE_PRESENT_POLL leaves presents to thread switches and draws,
as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
